2 CONT "In the Oulu City Council Court's court book for the year 1706 there is a "young boy" Jonas Andersson Diurman. Ubg rr 24.10.1706 p.231-234; 27.10.1706 p. 234-235; 17.11.1706 p. 236-237; 21.11.1706 p. 237-241 and p. 264.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that he was born in Umeå and 24 years old (born about 1682). His father was the master goldsmith Anders Diurman, residing in Jakobstad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonas had served for a few years with the regimental chaplain at the Royal Guard, later the dean and vicar in Karlshamn, Magister Olaus Orstadius. After that, for four years with the councilman Johan Sterner in Piteå. He had been 
2 CONC recommended for this position by his uncle, the dean of Skellefteå, Magister Buscherus. He had left Sterner's office in Stockholm in the summer of 1706.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Stockholm he had been allowed to accompany Matts Uhlander to Oulu and then travel on to his father in Jakobstad. He had become acquainted with Uhlander in Piteå. Uhlander was the son of Matts Henriksson's widow Catharina Lith
2 CONC ovius. In Oulu he had lived for a time with Catharina Lithovius but then had been given a job as a clerk with the commander Esajas Groop.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of 22.10.1706 he had committed a considerable theft. He had broken into a shed belonging to Claes Jenderjahn's widow through the roof. The shed contained property belonging to Catharina Lihtovius and the son of a merc
2 CONC hant from Stockholm, Carl Fagerholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT The stolen goods were valued at 773 dlr 19 ½ öre per kmt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonas confessed to the theft. Letters from Umeå and Piteå stated that he had no previous convictions and that there was only good things to say about him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonas was sentenced to death. The verdict was sent to the Royal Court of Appeal for consideration."
